 teach: {
  prompt: "Explain why four different gingival lumps (fibroma, pyogenic granuloma, peripheral giant cell granuloma, peripheral ossifying fibroma) share one treatment principle, and why some of them recur.",
  model: "All four are reactive hyperplasias: local irritation such as plaque, calculus or trauma drives the connective tissue to overproduce one component, collagen, granulation tissue, giant cells or mineralising fibroblasts. Because the stimulus is local, treatment is excision plus removal of the irritant, with histology to confirm which lesion it was and exclude anything worse. Lesions that arise deep in the periosteum or periodontal ligament, such as the peripheral ossifying fibroma and the giant cell granuloma, recur when the base is left or the calculus is not removed. So the excision goes down to periosteum or bone, the base is curetted, and the adjacent teeth are scaled."
 },
